            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The storm or tempest primarily symbolizes divine judgment, theophany (God's awe-inspiring presence), or the personal trials and tribulations of life.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The storm symbolizes the nations in turmoil, which Christ ultimately pacifies.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Divine judgment, theophany, or personal trials/tribulations.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The nations in turmoil.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book rejects the primary consensus views (divine judgment and personal trials), explicitly labeling them as merely 'commonly taught,' and reassigns the symbol to collective human geopolitical turmoil.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus understands storms in the Gospels as representing chaotic forces or the personal trials of believers, and Old Testament storms as God's judgment or presence. The book interprets the storm—even in the Gospel narratives of Christ stilling the waves—as a typological representation of the turbulent, warring nations.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By explicitly sidelining the primary consensus views ('Commonly taught: divine judgment, and the trials of life') in favor of identifying the storm with 'the nations in turmoil', the book presents a materially different referent. Although one consensus response notes political turmoil as a secondary prophetic motif, the book elevates this to the primary and universal meaning, fundamentally changing the interpretation of key passages like Mark 4:39.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"While Isaiah 17 and Psalm 65 explicitly link the roaring seas to the rushing of nations, applying this political/national referent directly to the Gospel accounts of the physical storm on the Sea of Galilee (Mark 4:39) may be seen as an imposition of prophetic metaphor onto historical narrative.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
